window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.cb=window.CHTML.cb||{};window.CHTML.cb[32]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Portfolio Credit Risk: Threshold Models, Mixtures, Large Portfolios and the Basel IRB Formula (MFE Sections 11.1–11.3)</h1>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">Source line — read first</div>\n<p data-b=\"2\"> <strong>Source actually read:</strong> McNeil, Frey and Embrechts (2015), Chapter 11: Sections 11.1 (threshold models, pp. 426–434), 11.2.1–11.2.2 (Bernoulli mixtures, pp. 436–438) and 11.3 (large portfolios and the Basel IRB formula, pp. 449–456). I did <em>not</em> study Poisson mixtures/CreditRisk<math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi></mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>, Monte Carlo (Section 11.4) or the statistical inference of Section 11.5. MFE’s Proposition 11.17 and 11.18 proofs are summarised; the Vasicek loss distribution and all the numerical tables are my own derivations and simulations, checked against MFE’s Table 11.1.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">Why a portfolio model at all</h2>\n<p data-b=\"4\">A bank’s loss is not the sum of independent defaults. Defaults cluster in bad years: they are driven by common factors (the economy, a policy shock, a commodity price). A portfolio model needs two ingredients: individual default probabilities and a rule for how defaults are linked. This chapter takes the <em>latent-variable</em> route inherited from Merton (Chapter 28) and shows that it equals a <em>conditional-independence</em> model, from which the Basel capital formula follows.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"5\" id=\"b5\">Notation and default correlation (pp. 426–428)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"6\"><math display=\"inline\"><mi>m</mi></math> obligors; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\"><mi>i</mi></math> defaults by <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>; number of defaults <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; loss <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> with exposure <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> and loss rate <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math>. The <em>default correlation</em> is the correlation of the indicators: <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>11.1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math> since <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>var</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math>. In the exchangeable case (all firms alike), with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>11.3</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Default correlations are tiny numbers even when the dependence is material; keep this in mind below.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"7\" id=\"b7\">Threshold models (pp. 426–431)</h2>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">Definition 11.1 (default-only form)</div>\n<p data-b=\"8\"> Default of obligor <math display=\"inline\"><mi>i</mi></math> occurs iff a critical variable <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<h5 data-b=\"9\" id=\"b9\">Lemma 11.2: the copula decides dependence.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"10\">Two threshold models are equivalent if the marginal default probabilities agree and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>X</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃</mo></mover></mrow></math> have the same copula <math display=\"inline\"><mi>C</mi></math>. Proof idea: the joint state distribution depends on the joint df of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>X</mi></math> at the thresholds; by Sklar’s theorem that is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. For a homogeneous group, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>…</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (11.5). <em>Consequence:</em> asset correlation is not default correlation; outside the normal family the correlation of the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>X</mi></math>’s does not even fix the default correlation (p. 428).</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"11\" id=\"b11\">Multivariate Merton <math display=\"inline\"><mo>⇒</mo></math> Gaussian threshold model (p. 430).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"12\">If <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math> are correlated lognormals, default <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invariant to monotone transformations, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mo>ln</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>ln</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>∼</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"13\" id=\"b13\">One-factor model (11.6, 11.8).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"14\"><span class=\"math display\">$$X_i=\\sqrt{\\beta_i}\\,F+\\sqrt{1-\\beta_i}\\,\\varepsilon_i,\\qquad F,\\varepsilon_i\\ \\text{iid }N(0,1).\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(32.1)}$$</span> <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the share of variance explained by the systematic factor <math display=\"inline\"><mi>F</mi></math>. If all <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></math> (equicorrelation), <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>corr</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box plainbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked check: asset correlation versus default correlation (my calculation)</div>\n<p data-b=\"15\"> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>, asset correlation <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>. <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.326</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0217</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math> (bivariate normal). So <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.000217</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1.18</mn><mi>%</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> An asset correlation of 12% gives a default correlation of only about 1.2%, yet it makes joint defaults <math display=\"inline\"><mn>2.2</mn></math> times as likely as under independence (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.0217</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math> against <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>). For <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math> the default correlation is 3.2%.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"16\" id=\"b16\">Bernoulli mixture models (pp. 436–438)</h2>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">Definition 11.5</div>\n<p data-b=\"17\"> Given a factor vector <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Ψ</mi></math>, defaults are <em>conditionally independent</em> Bernoulli variables with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Unconditionally, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"18\"><strong>Result 32.1 (Exchangeable mixtures, (11.14)–(11.15)).</strong> With <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>G</mi></math> its df: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Binomial</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>cov</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>var</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"19\"><em>Proof.</em> Given <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Q</mi></math> the indicators are independent with mean <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Q</mi></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>⋯</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>; take expectations. Then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>cov</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>var</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"20\"><em>Reading:</em> default correlation in a mixture model is always non-negative and equals the variance of the <em>conditional default probability</em>. If <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Q</mi></math> is constant, defaults are independent. Any <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math> can be reached by choosing <math display=\"inline\"><mi>G</mi></math>. Three popular choices (Example 11.6): beta, probit-normal <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, logit-normal. Sampling is easy in two stages: draw the factor, then independent Bernoulli draws.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"21\" id=\"b21\">Threshold models are mixture models (Section 11.2.4, applied on p. 455)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"22\">Take the one-factor Gaussian model, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></math> so that high <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Ψ</mi></math> means bad times. Given <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>, default occurs iff <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>β</mi></msqrt><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, i.e. <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>β</mi></msqrt><mi>f</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></math>. Hence <span class=\"math display\">$$p(\\psi)=\\Phi\\!\\Big(\\frac{\\Phi^{-1}(p)+\\sqrt{\\beta}\\,\\psi}{\\sqrt{1-\\beta}}\\Big)\\qquad(\\psi=-f).\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(32.2)}$$</span> This is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>β</mi><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>: the probit-normal mixture. The two model families coincide.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"23\" id=\"b23\">Large portfolios (pp. 449–454)</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"24\" id=\"b24\">The idea.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"25\">In a one-factor mixture with a huge number of small obligors, idiosyncratic risk averages out (law of large numbers) and <em>only the factor remains</em>: relative loss <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"26\"><strong>Result 32.2 (Proposition 11.17 (MFE)).</strong> Under (A1) conditional independence of the losses, (A2) <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∣</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and (A3) <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> almost surely.</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"27\"><em>Proof outline (MFE):</em> Conditional on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Ψ</mi></math> the <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>var</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math>. Petrov’s strong law for non-identically distributed variables, which needs <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mo>var</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math> (guaranteed by (A3)), gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; (A2) identifies the limit. Condition (A3) fails only if a few exposures dominate the book — <em>name concentration</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"28\"><strong>Result 32.3 (Proposition 11.18 (MFE)).</strong> If, in addition, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Ψ</mi></math> is scalar with df <math display=\"inline\"><mi>G</mi></math> strictly increasing at <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></math> is strictly increasing and continuous, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"29\"><em>Proof outline (MFE):</em> Almost-sure convergence implies convergence in distribution; by Lemma 11.19 quantiles converge at continuity points; and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for increasing <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></math>. <em>In words: the portfolio’s 99.9th percentile loss is the loss in the 99.9th percentile bad state of the economy.</em></p>\n<h5 data-b=\"30\" id=\"b30\">Vasicek’s limiting loss distribution (my derivation; MFE credit Vasicek 1997 on p. 456).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"31\">For equicorrelated Gaussian model with LGD 1, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mi>f</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. For <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>lim</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Check: with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>99.9</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.326</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.3464</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3.090</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>0.9381</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.339</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>9.03</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>. A 2000-firm Monte-Carlo gives 9.1% at 99.9% and 5.3% at 99% (limit: 5.25%), so the limit is accurate already for 2000 obligors.</p>\n<figure data-b=\"32\">\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/fig/f32_vas.svg\"/>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 32.1.</strong> Left: Vasicek limiting loss density for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math> at asset correlation 0.12 and 0.24; dotted lines mark the 99.9% quantiles (9.0% and 17.6%). Right: the 99.9% loss rate as a function of PD, against the PD itself. My calculation from the formulae above.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 data-b=\"33\" id=\"b33\">The Basel IRB formula, derived (pp. 455–456)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"34\">Basel requires 8% of risk-weighted assets, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">RWA</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Under the IRB approach the risk weight is <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.08</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>Φ</mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>11.36</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math> so the capital is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (11.37), with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.24</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> fixed by the regulator.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"35\" id=\"b35\">Derivation.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"36\">Use the one-factor Gaussian model (11.38) with portfolios built of a few homogeneous groups. By (11.35) with the conditional probability (32.2) evaluated at <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0.999</mn></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>: <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mn>0.999</mn></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>Φ</mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Each term is exactly (11.37) with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: the capital for obligor <math display=\"inline\"><mi>i</mi></math> is its <em>asymptotic contribution to the 99.9% VaR</em>, and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the asset correlation. Because the formula is a sum over obligors, capital is portfolio-invariant.</p>\n<table data-b=\"37\">\n<caption><strong>Table 32.1.</strong> Capital under the IRB formula, my computation. The last column subtracts expected loss, since I recall that the final Basel II requirement is net of expected loss; that detail is <strong>from my own knowledge of Basel II, not from MFE</strong> (MFE’s (11.37) has no such term). Doubling <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> from 0.12 to 0.24 nearly doubles capital; doubling the PD from 1% to 2% raises it by about 63%.</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">PD, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">99.9% default rate</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Capital per unit exposure, LGD 45%, from (11.37), <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\">Same, net of expected loss (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1%, 0.12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9.03%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">4.06%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.61%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1%, 0.24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">17.57%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7.91%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">7.46%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5%, 0.12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">27.02%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">12.16%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">9.91%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2%, 0.12</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">14.73%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">6.63%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">5.73%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">0.2%, 0.15</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3.41%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.53%</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1.44%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h5 data-b=\"38\" id=\"b38\">MFE’s criticisms (p. 456).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"39\">(i) <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> set ad hoc, independent of the portfolio; (ii) the group structure and one factor are oversimplified for international banks; (iii) based on an asymptotic result (no granularity adjustment); (iv) historical experience of the portfolio has no formal role. These should be weighed against its simplicity for supervisors. Banks’ economic-capital models are richer.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"40\" id=\"b40\">Model risk: the copula matters in the tail (Section 11.1.5, Table 11.1)</h2>\n<p data-b=\"41\">Gaussian and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math> threshold models with the same <math display=\"inline\"><mi>π</mi></math> and asset correlation <math display=\"inline\"><mi>ρ</mi></math> but different tail dependence: group A (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.06</mn><mi>%</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.58</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>), B (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mi>%</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3.8</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>), C (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7.5</mn><mi>%</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>9.21</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>); <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mn>000</mn></mrow></math> firms; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math> is Gaussian, <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>50</mn></msub></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></math> progressively heavier-tailed. The quantity is the number of defaults at the 95th and 99th percentile.</p>\n<table data-b=\"42\">\n<caption><strong>Table 32.2.</strong> MFE’s Table 11.1 (p. 434) and my independent replication (100 000 draws of the factor and a binomial number of defaults given it). My values match to within simulation error, so the effect is real: at group A the <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></math> model has about 6 times as many defaults at the 99th percentile as the Gaussian model (118 against 21), with identical default probabilities and asset correlation.</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"></th>\n<th colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0.95</mn></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></th>\n<th colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0.99</mn></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Group</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">50</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><math display=\"inline\"><mi>∞</mi></math></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">50</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A (MFE)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">23</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">21</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">49</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">118</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A (mine)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">23</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">24</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">20</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">49</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">119</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">B (MFE)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">109</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">153</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">239</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">157</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">261</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">589</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">B (mine)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">109</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">152</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">243</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">157</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">260</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">602</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C (MFE)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1618</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1723</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2085</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2206</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2400</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3067</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">C (mine)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1622</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">1722</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2068</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2218</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">2415</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">3032</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure data-b=\"43\">\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/fig/f32_t.svg\"/>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 32.2.</strong> Defaults among 10 000 firms at the 95th and 99th percentiles: Gaussian versus <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>50</mn></msub></math> versus <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></math> copulas (my replication of MFE Table 11.1, groups A and B).</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h5 data-b=\"44\" id=\"b44\">A related result (p. 450).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"45\">Once <math display=\"inline\"><mi>π</mi></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></math> are fixed, the beta, probit-normal, logit-normal and Clayton mixtures differ appreciably only beyond the 99th percentile of the mixing variable (Figure 11.2); so the choice of copula <em>family</em> matters mostly at extreme quantiles, while <math display=\"inline\"><mi>π</mi></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></math> themselves matter at all levels — and are hard to estimate.</p>\n<div class=\"box verdictbox\"><div class=\"bt\">Do you need the original?</div>\n<p data-b=\"46\"> <strong>For the models, proofs and the IRB derivation: no</strong> for the pages listed in the source line. <strong>Open MFE</strong> for Section 11.2.5 (CreditRisk<math display=\"inline\"><msup><mi></mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>), 11.4 (importance sampling) and 11.5 (estimating mixture models with data), which I did not study; and for the full proof of Lemma 11.19 (they cite Fristedt and Gray 1997 and Resnick 2008). <strong>Vasicek (1997/2002)</strong> is the source of the limit distribution; I derived it myself rather than read it.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"47\" id=\"b47\">What it means for the thesis (my inference)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p data-b=\"48\">The IRB formula is the bridge from credit risk to your dependent variable, capital adequacy: capital per unit exposure is a function <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Climate transition risk can enter through <strong>PD</strong> (borrowers’ fundamentals worsen), <strong>LGD</strong> (stranded collateral) and <strong><math display=\"inline\"><mi>ρ</mi></math></strong> (sectors become more exposed to a common policy factor).</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"49\">In Table 32.1, the <em>correlation channel</em> matters as much as the PD channel: moving <math display=\"inline\"><mi>ρ</mi></math> from 0.12 to 0.24 lifts capital by about 95%, more than moving PD from 1% to 2% (63%). A transition factor that raises common exposure is therefore a source of capital stress that PD-only stress tests miss.</p></li>\n<li><p data-b=\"50\">Since the regulatory <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> is fixed, the IRB approach does <em>not</em> automatically capture a rise in systematic exposure: only the standardised approach and internal models can. This is MFE’s criticism (i) applied to your setting.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"51\" id=\"b51\">Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qlist\">\n<p data-b=\"52\">Define default correlation and write it for an exchangeable portfolio.</p>\n<p data-b=\"53\">State the defining property of a Bernoulli mixture model.</p>\n<p data-b=\"54\">Write the IRB capital formula and name each piece.</p>\n<p data-b=\"55\">Why is asset correlation different from default correlation?</p>\n<p data-b=\"56\">Why is default correlation in an exchangeable mixture never negative?</p>\n<p data-b=\"57\">Why does a larger <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> raise capital?</p>\n<p data-b=\"58\">Why does the <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math> copula give many more defaults at the 99th percentile than the Gaussian copula with the same <math display=\"inline\"><mi>ρ</mi></math>?</p>\n<p data-b=\"59\">Compute the 99.9% conditional default rate for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"60\">With LGD 45% and exposure 100, what is the IRB capital for that obligor (ignore <math display=\"inline\"><mi>c</mi></math> and EL)?</p>\n<p data-b=\"61\">In an exchangeable model with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>, find <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"62\">Prove that <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"63\">Explain why the IRB formula allows capital to be computed obligor by obligor, and name two assumptions that make this work.</p>\n<p data-b=\"64\">How would you introduce a climate-transition factor into the Gaussian one-factor model and what would happen to the 99.9% loss?</p>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"65\" id=\"b65\">Answer sketches</h3>\n<div class=\"alist\">\n<p data-b=\"66\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msqrt><mi>⋯</mi></msqrt></mrow></math>; exchangeable: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"67\">Conditional on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Ψ</mi></math>, defaults are independent Bernoulli with probabilities <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"68\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Φ</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mi>δ</mi></math> LGD, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>e</mi></math> exposure, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>p</mi></math> PD, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> asset correlation, <math display=\"inline\"><mi>c</mi></math> technical factor.</p>\n<p data-b=\"69\">Asset correlation is for latent variables; default correlation is for 0/1 indicators and depends on the joint tail (copula) too.</p>\n<p data-b=\"70\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>cov</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>var</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"71\">A bigger <math display=\"inline\"><mi>β</mi></math> makes the loss in the bad state closer to a common shock, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> at the 99.9% factor level is larger.</p>\n<p data-b=\"72\">The <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math> copula has tail dependence: extreme low critical values occur together; the Gaussian copula does not.</p>\n<p data-b=\"73\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.02</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.054</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>0.15</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3873</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>0.85</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9220</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.999</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3.090</mn></mrow></math>: numerator <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.054</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1.197</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.857</mn></mrow></math>; divided by <math display=\"inline\"><mn>0.922</mn></math> gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.930</mn></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.930</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>17.6</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"74\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.45</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.1763</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>7.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"75\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.0003</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0001</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0001</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.02</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"76\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>⋯</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> by conditional independence; take expectations.</p>\n<p data-b=\"77\">The loss in the 99.9% state is a sum of individual conditional losses; needs the large-portfolio limit (idiosyncratic risk diversified) and a single factor with fixed correlation.</p>\n<p data-b=\"78\">Add <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>Ψ</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">climate</mtext></msub></math> with loadings by sector; the conditional PD of exposed sectors rises with the factor, raising <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mo>ℓ</mo><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0.999</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> because more variance is systematic.</p>\n</div>\n","s":[[222,"b3"],[222,"b5"],[223,"b7"],[223,"b16"],[224,"b21"],[224,"b23"],[225,"b33"],[226,"b40"],[227,"b47"]]};